            <ArticleTitle>The Osborn wave of hypothermia in normothermic patients.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>273-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The Osborn wave (also referred to as &quot;the J wave,&quot; &quot;the J deflection,&quot; or &quot;the camel's hump&quot;) is a distinctive deflection occurring at the QRS-ST junction of approximately 80% of hypothermic patients (core body temperature &lt; or = 95 degrees F). Generally, the amplitude and duration of Osborn waves are inversely related to core temperature. We report on eight normothermic patients whose 12-lead electrocardiograms demonstrated QRS-ST junction notches similar to those seen in hypothermia. These data support the concept that the Osborn wave is not pathognomonic of hypothermia.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
